    },
    {
      "title": "Program Representation Choices Shape Optimization Opportunities",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Representation Before Optimization",
          "body": "The sources share a concern with how programs are represented before optimization decisions are made. The profile-guided source reshapes hot control-flow paths into super-blocks and super-block loops, while the machine-learning source represents programs through quantifiable static, dynamic, or combined features."
        },
        {
          "title": "What Optimizers Can See",
          "body": "Super-block formation makes frequently executed paths easier for classic scalar optimizations to exploit. Feature engineering, feature selection, and dimension reduction make program behavior and structure available to learning algorithms without overwhelming models with redundant or sparse feature spaces."
        },
        {
          "title": "Structural and Statistical Views",
          "body": "The topic should preserve the distinction between structural compiler regions and statistical feature vectors, but can synthesize them as representation layers that determine what an optimizer can observe, compare, and act upon."
        }
      ],
