    },
    {
      "title": "Context-Dependent Optimization Tradeoffs",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "No Universally Best Optimization",
          "body": "Both sources emphasize that optimization choices are context dependent rather than universally beneficial. Profile-based classic optimizations target hot paths and complement conventional optimization, while machine-learning examples such as thread coarsening depend on program and hardware context."
        },
        {
          "title": "Competing Objectives",
          "body": "The profile-guided work manages tradeoffs such as executable-size growth while seeking speedups. The machine-learning work highlights tradeoffs among parallelism, register pressure, memory behavior, instruction-level parallelism, code size, speed, and power."
        },
        {
          "title": "Evidence for Payoff Decisions",
          "body": "This topic can frame compiler optimization as selecting transformations under competing objectives, with profiling inputs, benchmark measurements, and learned predictions used to identify when a transformation is likely to pay off."
        }
      ],
