    {
      "title": "From Hot-Path Mechanisms to Learned Optimization Policies",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Mechanisms for Exposing Hot Paths",
          "body": "The profile-guided source provides concrete mechanisms for exposing and optimizing hot paths: trace selection, tail duplication, eliminating entries into the middle of traces, and focusing optimization on frequently executed loop paths."
        },
        {
          "title": "Policy Learning Still Needs Compiler Machinery",
          "body": "The machine-learning source describes a broader policy-learning view in which models predict optimization decisions from program features, but it also states that learning cannot invent legal transformations or derive legality analyses by itself."
        },
        {
          "title": "Connecting Mechanism and Policy",
          "body": "A synthesis page could connect hand-engineered hot-path transformations with learned policies: profiles and features can guide when to apply transformations, while compiler-provided legality checks and transformation machinery remain necessary."
        }
      ],
